).  \nDenote the rows by \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) and the columns by \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection  \n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow \\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound – a construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) consider the two intervals of covered cells:\n\n* the **left interval**  \n  \\[\n  L_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\n  \\qquad\\text{(empty if }\\sigma(i)=1\\text{)},\n  \\]\n* the **right interval**  \n  \\[\n  R_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\},\n  \\qquad\\text{(empty if }\\sigma(i)=n\\text{)} .\n  \\]\n\nBoth \\(L_i\\) and \\(R_i\\) are contiguous horizontal strips of unit squares.  \nPlace a rectangular tile covering the whole strip \\(L_i\\) whenever \\(L_i\\neq\\varnothing\\); similarly place a tile covering the whole strip \\(R_i\\) whenever \\(R_i\\neq\\varnothing\\).  \n\nExactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) (its left interval is empty) and exactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\) (its right interval is empty).  \nHence the total number of tiles placed is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{non‑empty left intervals}}\n+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{non‑empty right intervals}}\n=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nAll tiles lie on grid lines, they are pairwise disjoint, and every row and column contains precisely one uncovered square.  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound – an inductive argument  \n\nWe prove that any admissible tiling uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nThe proof proceeds by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n**Base case \\(n=1\\).**  \nThe board consists of a single unit square, which must be uncovered; no tile is needed.  \nSince \\(2\\cdot1-2=0\\), the claim holds.\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the statement true for all boards of size \\((n-1)\\times (n-1)\\) with a permutation of uncovered squares.  \nConsider an \\(n\\times n\\) board with uncovered squares \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\nLet \\(r\\) be the unique row such that \\(\\sigma(r)=n\\); i.e., the uncovered square in column \\(n\\) lies in row \\(r\\).  \nRemove row \\(r\\) and column \\(n\\).  \nThe remaining \\((n-1)\\times (n-1)\\) board still has exactly one uncovered square in each of its rows and columns (the restriction of \\(\\sigma\\) to the remaining indices is a bijection).  \nBy the induction hypothesis, any tiling of this reduced board needs at least  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)-2=2n-4\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\nNow examine the cells that were removed:\n\n* **Row \\(r\\).** Its right interval is empty (the uncovered square is at column \\(n\\)).  \n  Its left interval \\(L_r=\\{(r,j)\\mid 1\\le j<n\\}\\) is a contiguous horizontal strip of length \\(n-1\\).  \n  Any tile covering a cell of \\(L_r\\) cannot extend past column \\(n\\) (otherwise it would contain the uncovered square), hence a single tile is necessary to cover all of \\(L_r\\).\n\n* **Column \\(n\\).** Apart from the uncovered square \\((r,n)\\), column \\(n\\) consists of two vertical strips:  \n  \\[\n  T=\\{(i,n)\\mid 1\\le i<r\\},\\qquad\n  B=\\{(i,n)\\mid r<i\\le n\\}.\n  \\]\n  Each of \\(T\\) and \\(B\\) is a contiguous vertical segment (possibly empty).  \n  Any tile covering a cell of \\(T\\) (resp. \\(B\\)) cannot cross the uncovered square, so each non‑empty segment requires at least one tile.\n\nAt least two of the three sets \\(\\{L_r,T,B\\}\\) are non‑empty:  \nif \\(r=1\\) then \\(T\\) is empty but \\(L_r\\) and \\(B\\) are non‑empty;  \nif \\(r=n\\) then \\(B\\) is empty but \\(L_r\\) and \\(T\\) are non‑empty;  \nif \\(1<r<n\\) then all three are non‑empty.  \nConsequently the removed 