left\u2011hand cell, but never with both.\n\n5. **Counting tiles.**  \n   The \\(n-1\\) distinct left\u2011side columns required by the right\u2011hand cells must belong to \\(n-1\\) different tiles, and the \\(n-1\\) distinct right\u2011side columns required by the left\u2011hand cells must belong to another \\(n-1\\) different tiles (by Lemma\u202f3). Hence any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   tiles.\n\n6. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  \n\n   *Horizontal tiles.* For each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a horizontal rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) covering the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j>i\\).  \n\n   *Vertical tiles.* For each column \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a vertical rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\) covering the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\).  \n\n   The \\(n-1\\) horizontal and the \\(n-1\\) vertical rectangles are pairwise disjoint, together they cover every unit square except the diagonal ones, and their total number is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same number, therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote the set of unit squares of the board by  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered square, there is a function  \n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \nThe same condition for columns forces \\(\\pi\\) to be a bijection; hence \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Two families of forced cells  \n\nFor every row \\(i\\) with \\(\\pi(i)<n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nr_i=(i,\\pi(i)+1) \\qquad\\text{(the cell immediately to the right of the hole).}\n\\]\n\nFor every row \\(i\\) with \\(\\pi(i)>1\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1) \\qquad\\text{(the cell immediately to the left of the hole).}\n\\]\n\nThus we have the two sets  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{\\,r_i\\mid \\pi(i)<n\\,\\},\\qquad\nL=\\{\\,\\ell_i\\mid \\pi(i)>1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth sets contain exactly \\(n-1\\) cells, because the values \\(\\pi(i)\\) run through all numbers \\(1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Geometry of a tile that contains a forced cell  \n\nA **tile** is an axis\u2011parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines and whose interior consists of unit squares.  \n\n*Lemma 1.* If a tile \\(T\\) contains a cell \\(r_i\\) then the left side of \\(T\\) lies on the vertical grid line \\(x=\\pi(i)+1\\).\n\n*Proof.* Suppose the left side of \\(T\\) were at a column \\(\\le\\pi(i)\\). Then the tile would also contain the square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\), i.e. the hole of row \\(i\\), which is prohibited. Hence the leftmost column of \\(T\\) is exactly \\(\\pi(i)+1\\). \u220e  \n\n*Lemma 2.* If a tile \\(T\\) contains a cell \\(\\ell_i\\) then the right side of \\(T\\) lies on the vertical grid line \\(x=\\pi(i)-1\\).\n\n*Proof.* Symmetric to Lemma\u202f1. \u220e  \n\nConsequences:\n\n* The set of left\u2011side columns of tiles that cover the cells of \\(R\\) is precisely \\(\\{\\,\\pi(i)+1\\mid \\pi(i)<n\\,\\}= \\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n* The set of right\u2011side columns of tiles that cover the cells of \\(L\\) is precisely \\(\\{\\,\\pi(i)-1\\mid \\pi(i)>1\\,\\}= \\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A tile cannot serve both a right\u2011hand and a left\u2011hand cell  \n\n*Lemma 3.* No tile can contain simultaneously a cell of \\(R\\) and a cell of \\(L\\).\n\n*Proof.* Assume a tile \\(T\\) contains \\(r_i\\) and \\(\\ell_j\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 the left side of \\(T\\) is the line \\(x=\\pi(i)+1\\); by Lemma\u202f2 the right side of \\(T\\) is the line \\(x=\\pi(j)-1\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(i)+1\\le \\pi(j)-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe tile also contains every row between its top and bottom edges; in particular it contains all rows whose indices lie between \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) (inclusive). For any such row \\(k\\) the hole \\((k,\\pi(k))\\) must lie **outside** the column interval \\([\\pi(i)+1,\\;\\pi(j)-1]\\); otherwise the tile would cover a hole, contradicting the problem statement.\n\nConsider the set \\(S=\\{\\pi(k)\\mid \\min\\{i,j\\}\\le k\\le \\max\\{i,j\\}\\}\\).  \nBecause \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, \\(|S| = |\\,\\{k\\mid \\min\\{i,j\\}\\le k\\le \\max\\{i,j\\}\\}\\,| = |j-i|+1\\).\n\nThe interval \\([\\pi(i)+1,\\;\\pi(j)-1]\\) contains exactly \\(\\pi(j)-\\pi(i)-1\\) integer columns.  \nIf (1) were a strict inequality, i.e. \\(\\pi(j)-\\pi(i)-1\\ge 1\\), then the number of columns in the interval would be at least \\(1\\). Since the rows in the interval are at least two (otherwise \\(i=j\\) and the tile would have zero width), we would have  \n\n\\[\n|S| = |j-i|+1 \\le \\pi(j)-\\pi(i)-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich forces some \\(\\pi(k)\\in S\\) to lie inside the interval, contradicting the fact that the tile cannot contain a hole.  \n\nThus the only possibility is that the interval is empty, i.e. \\(\\pi(j)-\\pi(i)-1=0\\). Then \\(\\pi(j)=\\pi(i)+1\\) and the left and right sides of the tile coincide, giving a rectangle of zero width \u2013 not a legitimate tile.  \n\nHence a contradiction arises in every case, proving that no tile can contain both a cell of \\(R\\) and a cell of \\(L\\). \u220e  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nThe \\(n-1\\) distinct left\u2011side columns required by the cells of \\(R\\) must belong to \\(n-1\\) different tiles (otherwise two cells of \\(R\\) would share the same left side, contradicting Lemma\u202f1).  \nSimilarly, the \\(n-1\\) distinct right\u2011side columns required by the cells of \\(L\\) must belong to another \\(n-1\\) different tiles (Lemma\u202f2).  \n\nLemma\u202f3 guarantees that the two families of tiles are disjoint. Consequently any tiling satisfying the condition uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Horizontal tiles.* For each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\).  \n\n*Vertical tiles.* For each column \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\((n-j)\\times 1\\).\n\nThe \\(n-1\\) horizontal tiles are pairwise disjoint, as are the \\(n-1\\) vertical tiles, and a horizontal tile never meets a vertical tile because they lie strictly above or strictly to the right of the diagonal, respectively. Together they cover every unit square of the board except the diagonal ones, and the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nBoth the lower bound and the explicit construction give the same number of tiles. Therefore the minimum possible number of rectangular tiles that Matilda must place is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
