t construction coincide, therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nHence the set \\(U\\) of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation\n\\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(r_j\\) the unique row index with \\((r_j,j)\\in U\\); thus \\(\\sigma(r_j)=j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Lower bound\n\nConsider a fixed column \\(j\\).\n\n*If \\(r_j\\neq 1\\)*, the square \\((r_j-1,j)\\) lies directly **above** the uncovered square.  \n*If \\(r_j\\neq n\\)*, the square \\((r_j+1,j)\\) lies directly **below** the uncovered square.\n\nAny axis\u2011parallel rectangle that contains both of these two squares must also contain the whole vertical segment between them, in particular the square \\((r_j,j)\\), which is forbidden because it is uncovered.  \nTherefore the two squares \\((r_j-1,j)\\) (when it exists) and \\((r_j+1,j)\\) (when it exists) must belong to **different** rectangles.\n\nConsequently each column contributes two distinct rectangles, except:\n\n* the column whose uncovered square is in the top row (\\(r_j=1\\)) contributes only the rectangle below it, and  \n* the column whose uncovered square is in the bottom row (\\(r_j=n\\)) contributes only the rectangle above it.\n\nSince exactly one column has \\(r_j=1\\) and exactly one column has \\(r_j=n\\), the total number of rectangles needed is at least  \n\n\\[\n2n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Construction achieving the bound\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); i.e., the uncovered squares are the main diagonal\n\\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) define two rectangles:\n\n* **Upper rectangle** \\(R_j^{\\text{up}}\\): the set of squares \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\}\\) (empty when \\(j=1\\));\n* **Lower rectangle** \\(R_j^{\\text{down}}\\): the set of squares \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\}\\) (empty when \\(j=n\\)).\n\nEach \\(R_j^{\\text{up}}\\) and \\(R_j^{\\text{down}}\\) is a vertical strip of width \\(1\\) and height respectively \\(j-1\\) and \\(n-j\\).  \nBecause the uncovered square in column \\(j\\) is \\((j,j)\\), none of these strips contains a forbidden square.  \n\nThe strips belonging to distinct columns are disjoint, so the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{T}= \\{R_j^{\\text{up}}\\mid j=2,\\dots ,n\\}\\cup\\{R_j^{\\text{down}}\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every unit square of the board except the diagonal squares.  \n\nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{T}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Minimality\n\nEquation (1) gives a universal lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles for any admissible placement.  \nEquation (2) exhibits a placement that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nHence the lower bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot 2025 - 2 = 4050 - 2 = \\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
