rom different columns, nor two lower cells from different columns. They argue that because the rows r_j-1 are distinct, a rectangle covering two upper cells would have to have row interval covering both rows, but then its bottom row would be at most the minimum of those rows; but then the rectangle would not include the upper cell with larger row index? Actually need to examine: Suppose rectangle R contains upper cells u_{j1} and u_{j2} with rows r_{j1}-1 and r_{j2}-1. If r_{j1}-1 < r_{j2}-1 (i.e., j1's upper cell is higher), then rectangle's top row must be at least max of those rows? Wait rectangle orientation: vertical rectangle includes contiguous rows from some top row to some bottom row. If it contains both cells, then its row interval must include both rows, i.e., from min to max. So top row ≤ min(r_{j1}-1, r_{j2}-1) and bottom row ≥ max(r_{j1}-1, r_{j2}-1). But they claim bottom row is at most min? That seems contradictory. Let's parse: They said: \"Then the row interval of R must contain both rows r_{j1}-1 and r_{j2}-1; consequently its bottom row is at most min{r_{j1}-1, r_{j2}-1}.\" Actually bottom row is the highest row index (since rows increase downward). If we number rows from top to bottom (1 at top), then \"bottom row\" is the larger index. So bottom row ≥ max. So the claim that bottom row is at most min is wrong. Let's examine orientation: Usually row index increases downward. So row 1 is top, row n is bottom. Upper cell is above the uncovered square, so its row index is r_j-1 (one less). Lower cell is r_j+1 (one more). So for upper cells, the row index is smaller than the uncovered square's row. So if we have two upper cells from different columns, they have row indices r_{j1}-1 and r_{j2}-1. The rectangle that contains both must have its top row ≤ min(r_{j1}-1, r_{j2}-1) and bottom row ≥ max(r_{j1}-1, r_{j2}-1). So the rectangle's row interval must span from the smaller index to the larger index. So bottom row is at least max, not at most min. So