otherwise it would have to cover a row where column \\(c\\) is absent \u2013 which would contain the uncovered square of that row, contradicting the rules.  \n   Hence at least \\(b_c\\) distinct rectangles are needed to cover the cells of column \\(c\\) in \\(L\\). Summing over all columns,\n\n   \\[\n   \\#\\text{rectangles covering }L\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1} b_c .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   **Lemma 1.** \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1} b_c \\ge n-1\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Scan the rows from \\(i=1\\) to \\(i=n\\). For each adjacent pair \\((i,i+1)\\) consider the change \\(\\Delta_i=|\\pi(i+1)-\\pi(i)|\\).  \n   If \\(\\pi(i+1)>\\pi(i)\\), then for every column \\(c\\) with \\(\\pi(i)<c\\le\\pi(i+1)\\) the binary sequence \\(f_c\\) switches from 0 at row \\(i\\) to 1 at row \\(i+1\\); this creates a **new run** of 1\u2019s in column \\(c\\).  \n   If \\(\\pi(i+1)<\\pi(i)\\), then for every column \\(c\\) with \\(\\pi(i+1)<c\\le\\pi(i)\\) the sequence switches from 1 to 0, ending a run and simultaneously starting a new run at the next row. In both cases exactly \\(|\\pi(i+1)-\\pi(i)|\\) new runs appear across the columns.  \n\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c=1}^{n-1} b_c\n   =\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} |\\pi(i+1)-\\pi(i)|.\n   \\]\n\n   The right\u2011hand sum is minimized when the permutation is monotone increasing, i.e. \\(\\pi(i)=i\\), giving \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}1=n-1\\). For any other permutation at least one difference exceeds 1, so the sum is \\(\\ge n-1\\). \u220e\n\n   Combining (1) with Lemma\u202f1 yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\#\\text{rectangles covering }L\\;\\ge\\;n-1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *For the right part.*  \n   Apply the same argument to the reflected board (replace column \\(j\\) by \\(n+1-j\\)). The right part \\(R\\) also needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  \n\n   Consequently any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   rectangles. This is the lower bound (3).\n\n5. **Conclusion** \u2013  \n   The construction of step\u202f3 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, and the lower bound (3) shows that fewer are impossible. Hence the minimum number of tiles is precisely \\(2n-2\\).  \n\n   For the original board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048}.\n   \\]\n\n   An explicit tiling attaining the bound is described in the construction: uncovered squares on the main diagonal, vertical strips below it, and horizontal strips above it.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of axis\u2011parallel rectangular tiles in which each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square forces the set of uncovered squares to be a **permutation matrix**: there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j>\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) consists of all cells strictly to the left of the uncovered squares, \\(R\\) those strictly to the right.  The two sets are disjoint and together contain every covered cell.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Upper bound \u2013 an explicit tiling\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\)).\n\n*Left part.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid i=c+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_c\\) is a vertical rectangle of size \\((n-c)\\times1\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{T_c\\}_{c=1}^{n-1}\\) covers exactly the cells of \\(L\\) and the rectangles are pairwise disjoint.\n\n*Right part.*  \nFor each row \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n-1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nH_r=\\{(r,j)\\mid j=r+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_r\\) is a horizontal rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-r)\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{H_r\\}_{r=1}^{n-1}\\) covers exactly the cells of \\(R\\) and the rectangles are pairwise disjoint.\n\nThus the whole board (except the diagonal holes) is tiled with  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles. Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Lower bound \u2013 any tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles\n\nFix an arbitrary admissible tiling and the associated permutation \\(\\pi\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.** The left part \\(L\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a fixed column \\(c\\;(1\\le c\\le n-1)\\) consider the binary sequence  \n\n\\[\nf_c(i)=\\begin{cases}\n1,&\\text{if }\\pi(i)>c,\\\\[2pt]\n0,&\\text{if }\\pi(i)\\le c,\n\\end{cases}\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nThe rows that contain a cell of \\(L\\) in column \\(c\\) are precisely those with \\(f_c(i)=1\\).  \nWrite these rows as a union of maximal consecutive blocks (runs of 1\u2019s); let the number of such runs be \\(b_c\\).\n\nA rectangle intersecting column \\(c\\) must be contained wholly inside a single run, because if it spanned two distinct runs it would have to include a row where column \\(c\\) is absent \u2013 that row contains the uncovered square of its column, which a tile may not cover. Hence at least \\(b_c\\) distinct rectangles are needed to cover the cells of column \\(c\\) in \\(L\\).\n\nSumming over all columns gives  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{rectangles covering }L\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1} b_c .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nNow examine how the runs appear when we move from row \\(i\\) to row \\(i+1\\).  \nIf \\(\\pi(i+1)>\\pi(i)\\) then for each column \\(c\\) with \\(\\pi(i)<c\\le\\pi(i+1)\\) the value of \\(f_c\\) changes from \\(0\\) at row \\(i\\) to \\(1\\) at row \\(i+1\\); this creates a new run of 1\u2019s in column \\(c\\).  \nIf \\(\\pi(i+1)<\\pi(i)\\) then for each column \\(c\\) with \\(\\pi(i+1)<c\\le\\pi(i)\\) the value of \\(f_c\\) changes from \\(1\\) to \\(0\\); the run of 1\u2019s that ended at row \\(i\\) is followed by a new run (starting at row \\(i+1\\)). In both cases exactly \\(|\\pi(i+1)-\\pi(i)|\\) new runs appear across the columns.\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1} b_c\n   =\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} |\\pi(i+1)-\\pi(i)|.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe right\u2011hand sum is minimized when the permutation is monotone increasing, i.e. \\(\\pi(i)=i\\), giving \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}1=n-1\\). For any other permutation at least one difference exceeds \\(1\\), so the sum is \\(\\ge n-1\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1} b_c \\ge n-1 .\n\\]\n\nTogether with (5) this yields the desired bound \\(\\#\\text{rectangles covering }L\\ge n-1\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.** The right part \\(R\\) also requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nReflect the board horizontally (replace column \\(j\\) by column \\(n+1-j\\)). Under this reflection the right part \\(R\\) becomes a left part of the reflected board, to which Lemma\u202f1 applies. Hence the same lower bound holds for \\(R\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nAdding the two lemmas we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\text{any admissible tiling uses at least }(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\text{ rectangles}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Completion of the proof\n\nFrom the explicit construction (4) we have a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, and from (7) no tiling can use fewer. Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is precisely  \n\n\\[\n2n-2.\n\\]\n\nFor the original problem \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Explicit example for the \\(2025\\times2025\\) board**\n\n* Uncovered squares: the main diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,2025\\).  \n* Tiles below the diagonal (vertical): for each column \\(c=1,\\dots ,2024\\) place the rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  T_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid i=c+1,\\dots ,2025\\},\n  \\]\n\n  of size \\((2025-c)\\times1\\).\n\n* Tiles above the diagonal (horizontal): for each row \\(r=1,\\dots ,2024\\) place the rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  H_r=\\{(r,j)\\mid j=r+1,\\dots ,2025\\},\n  \\]\n\n  of size \\(1\\times(2025-r)\\).\n\nThese \\(2024+2024=4048\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint, avoid the diagonal holes, and satisfy the requirement that each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square. Hence the bound is attainable."
